{
  "answer": "In Shakespeare's *Hamlet*, the prince's hesitation is not simply cowardice but a profound meditation on mortality that ultimately paralyzes his performance of revenge. Hamlet delays killing Claudius not because he lacks will, but because he is overwhelmed by the consequences of death and the moral theater of his own role. Early in the play, he laments that conscience—meaning thought or reflection—'does make cowards of us all,' showing how intellectualizing death (the 'undiscover'd country') turns decisive action into paralyzed performance. Later, when he stabs Polonius through the arras, he dismissively calls him a 'rash, intruding fool,' exposing a brutal contrast: while Hamlet hesitates to confront his king, he kills a lesser man impulsively, revealing that his hesitation is selective, tied to the gravity of the target's mortality. These two moments together frame hesitation not as simple delay but as the central performance of a mind caught between the certainty of death and the uncertainty of just action. The classroom should see Hamlet's famous soliloquy and the closet scene as bookends of this conflict.",
