{
  "answer": "In Hamlet, hesitation is not merely indecision but a profound confrontation with mortality that paralyzes performance. Hamlet's famous soliloquy, 'To be, or not to be, that is the question,' captures his agonized weighing of life's suffering against the unknown of death. He hesitates because he fears 'what dreams may come' after death, revealing that mortality itself fuels his delay. Later, when the Ghost beckons, Hamlet suddenly shifts from paralysis to reckless action, declaring 'My fate cries out, and makes each petty artery in this body as hardy as the Nemean lion’s nerve.' This shows that the command to revenge momentarily overrides his fear, yet his performance remains impulsive and erratic. Thus, Hamlet's hesitation stems from an existential dread of death, and his sporadic bursts of action prove unsustainable—suggesting that true performance (whether as a revenger or a sane prince) falters when mortality looms. The play demonstrates that confronting death can either paralyze or radicalize, but rarely produces clear, steady action.",
