{
  "answer": "In Hamlet, Shakespeare intertwines hesitation, mortality, and performance to reveal how paralysis before death can become a tragic performance in itself. The play's most famous hesitation occurs in the 'To be or not to be' soliloquy, where Hamlet weighs the pain of living against the uncertainty of death. He asks, 'Whether 'tis nobler in the mind to suffer / The slings and arrows of outrageous fortune'—a hesitation that is itself a performed contemplation, delaying action while exploring mortality. Yet Hamlet also critiques such performative deliberation. When he watches an actor weep for Hecuba, he rebukes himself: 'What would he do, / Had he the motive and the cue for passion / That I have?' Here, Hamlet admits his own hesitation is a kind of failed performance: others act while he only rehearses. Mortality drives this crisis; the fear of 'the dread of something after death' (imagined in the same soliloquy) paralyzes him. Ultimately, Hamlet's delay is his most defining performance, a tragic refusal to act until death forces his hand.",
